workedExamples.question_text:
The $y$-intercept and gradient step of a line are shown. Write its equation.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[<->,black] (-1,0)--(5,0);
\draw[<->,black] (0,-2)--(0,4);
\path (5,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,4) node[above] {$y$};
\fill[black] (0,3) circle (1.8pt) (4,1) circle (1.8pt);
\path (0,3) node[left] {$(0,3)$};
\draw[dashed,black] (0,3)--(4,3)--(4,1);
\path (2,3) node[above] {right $4$} (4,2) node[right] {down $2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The $y$-intercept is $(0,3)$, so $c=3$. Moving left to right, the rise is $-2$ and the run is $4$.
$\begin{align*}m&=\frac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}}\\m&=\frac{-2}{4}\\m&=-\frac{1}{2}\end{align*}$
Substitute into $y=mx+c$:
$y=-\frac{1}{2}x+3$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{solutionblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{stepred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[<->,black] (-1,0)--(5,0);
\draw[<->,black] (0,-2)--(0,4);
\path (5,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[<->,thick,solutionblue] (-0.6,3.3)--(4.6,0.7);
\fill[black] (0,3) circle (1.8pt) (4,1) circle (1.8pt);
\path (0,3) node[left] {$(0,3)$};
\draw[dashed,stepred] (0,3)--(4,3)--(4,1);
\path (2,3) node[above] {run $4$} (4,2) node[right] {rise $-2$};
\path (2,1.5) node[below,text=solutionblue] {$y=-\frac{1}{2}x+3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
